# Line endings.
#
# Orbit runs on Linux and is edited from Windows — a WSL shell against a clone
# living on the Windows filesystem is a normal way to work on it. Without this
# file that is a trap: a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites every
# file with CRLF, git reports the entire repository as modified, and `git switch`
# refuses to move because it would "overwrite local changes" that are not changes.
#
# LF in the working tree, on every platform. This is not a preference — three
# things in here break outright on CRLF:
#
#   *.sh   a shebang followed by \r gives `/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file
#          or directory`, which names the interpreter and not the real problem
#   *.sql  psql passes the \r through into string literals and function bodies
#   *.mjs  scripts/smoke.mjs is run by node directly
#
# Windows-native scripts are the one exception: PowerShell wants CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Never touch these: a "line ending fix" applied to a binary corrupts it.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf  binary

# Generated. Kept in the repository, but there is no value in reading its diff.
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff linguist-generated
